ct",9,1,19]],"327":[["Act",9,20,31]],"328":[["Act",9,32,43]],"329":[["Act",10,1,33]],"330":[["Act",10,34,48]],"331":[["Act",11,1,18]],"332":[["Act",11,19,30]],"333":[["Act",12,1,2]],"334":[["Act",12,3,5]],"335":[["Act",12,6,19]],"336":[["Act",12,20,23]],"337":[["Act",12,24,25],["Act",13,1,3]],"338":[["Act",12,24,25],["Act",13,1,52],["Act",14,1,28]],"339":[["Act",13,4,12]],"340":[["Act",13,13,52]],"341":[["Act",14,1,5]],"342":[["Act",14,6,20]],"343":[["Act",14,21,28]],"344":[["Act",15,1,34]],"345":[["Act",15,1,41],["Act",16,1,40],["Act",17,1,34],["Act",18,1,23]],"346":[["Act",15,35,39]],"348":[["Act",15,40,41]],"349":[["Act",16,1,5]],"350":[["Act",16,6,8]],"351":[["Act",16,9,12]],"352":[["Act",16,13,15]],"353":[["Act",16,16,18]],"354":[["Act",16,19,34]],"355":[["Act",16,25,34]],"356":[["Act",16,35,40]],"357":[["Act",17,1,9]],"358":[["Act",17,10,15]],"359":[["Act",17,16,34]],"360":[["Act",18,1,17]],"347":[["Gal",1,1,1]],"361":[["Act",18,18,23]],"362":[["Act",18,24,28]],"363":[["Act",18,24,28],["Act",19,1,41],["Act",20,1,16]],"364":[["Act",19,1,22]],"365":[["Act",19,23,41]],"366":[["Act",20,1,6]],"367":[["Act",20,7,12]],"368":[["Act",20,13,16]],"369":[["Act",20,17,38]],"370":[["Act",21,1,17]],"371":[["Act",21,18,26]],"372":[["Act",21,27,36]],"373":[["Act",21,37,40],["Act",22,1,29]],"374":[["Act",22,30,30],["Act",23,1,11]],"375":[["Act",23,12,22]],"376":[["Act",23,23,35],["Act",24,1,27]],"377":[["Act",25,1,12]],"378":[["Act",25,13,21]],"379":[["Act",25,22,27],["Act",26,1,32]],"380":[["Act",27,1,12]],"381":[["Act",27,1,44],["Act",28,1,31]],"382":[["Act",27,13,44]],"383":[["Act",28,1,10]],"384":[["Act",28,11,16]],"385":[["Act",28,17,31]]},"notes":[{"heading":"Why the dates differ","body":"There is no single biblical chronology, and any app that shows you one date without saying whose it is has made a choice on your behalf.\n\nThe disagreements come from three places. First, the manuscript traditions differ: the Hebrew Masoretic Text, the Greek Septuagint and the Samaritan Pentateuch give different ages in the genealogies of Genesis 5 and 11, and the Septuagint adds an ancestor after the flood. Those differences alone move creation by well over a thousand years.\n\nSecond, chronologists disagree about whether the genealogies are continuous. Read as an unbroken father-to-son chain, they yield a creation around 4000 BC. Read as a selective list that skips generations - a normal practice in ancient genealogy - they yield no total at all.\n\nThird, the regnal records in Kings and Chronicles are genuinely hard. Reigns are counted differently in Israel and Judah, co-regencies overlap, and the two books sometimes disagree. Reconciling them is the problem Edwin Thiele spent a career on."},{"heading":"What is actually fixed","body":"Not everything is contested. From the divided monarchy onward, the biblical narrative can be tied to Assyrian and Babylonian records that carry their own dates, and the agreement is broadly good.\n\nThe later the event, the firmer the ground. The fall of Jerusalem to Babylon and the edict of Cyrus permitting the return are fixed within a year or two by external sources, and essentially every scheme agrees about them. The uncertainty is concentrated in the primeval history and the patriarchs, where there is nothing outside the text to check against."},{"heading":"Why some schemes show no date","body":"The event timeline in this app was compiled under one scheme, Floyd Nolen Jones's, so that is the only one with a date for all 450 events. Other schemes carry only the dates that can be traced to a published statement of that chronology.\n\nWhere a scheme has no date for an event, the app says so. It does not quietly fall back to another scheme's number and present it as that scheme's answer. A blank is information."},{"heading":"Which should you use","body":"If you want the chronology printed in the margins of a King James Bible, choose Ussher. If you want a modern conservative reckoning of the same kind, Jones is the default here and differs from Ussher by only a year or two at most points.\n\nIf you are reading alongside academic commentary, the critical scheme will match what you find there, at the cost of offering no dates for the earliest chapters. None of these is the app's opinion. The choice is yours and you can change it at any time."}],"schemes":["jones","critical","samaritan","septuagint","thiele","ussher"]}